	title: "About",
	description: "About Evey Huang",
};
---

<PageLayout meta={meta}>
	<section>
		<div class="section-head">
			<h1>About</h1>
			<p class="section-label">Bio</p>
		</div>
		<div class="max-w-3xl space-y-5">
			<p>
				I am Evey Huang, a Research Assistant Professor at Northwestern University’s Kellogg School of Management. I study human–AI collaboration in organizations across science, entrepreneurship, and knowledge work domains. 
			</p>
			<p>
				Organizations play a powerful role in shaping how people develop and which opportunities become available to them. 
				My research asks: as AI becomes embedded in organizations, how do choices about its design and 
				use change how people learn, what organizations see, and how organizations make judgments? 
			</p>	
			<p>	
				My work takes a mixed-methods, interdisciplinary approach and leverages in-depth qualitative investigations, computational social science, system design and field deployment, and AI-enabled measurement.
				I contribute empirical accounts of how people learn and how organizational judgments form, 
				evidence about how AI changes these processes and their outcomes, and methods and interventions 
				for studying and supporting them at scale. 
			</p>
			<p>
				I earned my PhD in Technology and Social Behavior at Northwestern, a joint program in
				Computer Science and Communication Studies. My doctoral research grew out of a sustained
				partnership with an entrepreneurship incubator. I also coached undergraduate engineering
				students in human-centered design and engineering practices.
			</p>
			<p>
				Alongside academic research, I contribute to AI design and engineering projects in industry.
				Outside work, I enjoy guitar, photography, tennis, reading, doodling, and cooking spicy food.
			</p>
			<p><a class="cactus-link" href="/posts">Explore my research</a> · <a class="cactus-link" href="/resume">View my CV</a></p>
		</div>
	</section>
</PageLayout>
